        "title": "Canon Formation and Literary Institutions",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "The literary canon is the set of texts that a culture treats as centrally important — that universities teach, that critics discuss, that educated people are expected to know. It is not a list inscribed in nature; it is a social artifact produced and maintained by a set of institutions. Understanding how canons form is equivalent to understanding how standards, protocols, and dominant paradigms form in any field."
